guides.body:
> **In plain words:** Every request travels DNS → TCP → TLS → load balancer or proxy → app. When something is slow or failing, walk that path step by step and check each hop.

## Key concepts

| Concept | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| **OSI / TCP-IP layers** | L3 IP routing, L4 TCP/UDP ports, L7 HTTP. An L4 load balancer forwards connections; an L7 one reads HTTP (host, path, headers). |
| **TCP handshake** | SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK, then TLS handshake, then HTTP. Each round trip adds latency; keep-alive and HTTP/2 reuse connections. |
| **DNS records** | A/AAAA (IP), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), TXT (verification, SPF), NS (delegation). TTL controls caching: lower it before a migration. |
| **TLS** | Encrypts traffic and proves the server's identity with a certificate chain. Let's Encrypt issues free 90-day certificates via ACME. |
| **Reverse proxy** | Sits in front of apps: TLS termination, routing by host/path, caching, compression, rate limits, header rewriting. |
| **CIDR & subnets** | 10.0.0.0/16 = 65,536 addresses. Public subnets route to an internet gateway; private subnets go out through a NAT gateway. |
| **502 vs 504** | 502 Bad Gateway: the upstream refused or broke the connection. 504 Gateway Timeout: the upstream did not answer in time. |
| **Firewalls** | Host: ufw/iptables/nftables. Cloud: security groups (stateful, per instance) and NACLs (stateless, per subnet). |

## Best practices

- ✅ Terminate TLS at the edge with automatic renewal (Let's Encrypt / ACM)
- ✅ Put nginx or a load balancer in front of every app; never expose app ports directly
- ✅ Set timeouts consistently: client < proxy < upstream keep-alive
- ✅ Rate-limit login and API paths
- ✅ Keep databases in private subnets with no public IP

## Common mistakes to avoid

- ❌ Opening 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH or database ports
- ❌ Mismatched keep-alive timeouts causing random 502s
- ❌ Forgetting to forward `Host` and `X-Forwarded-*` headers
- ❌ Low DNS TTL never set before a migration

## Setup & commands

### nginx reverse proxy with TLS

```
# /etc/nginx/conf.d/app.conf
upstream app { server 127.0.0.1:8000; keepalive 32; }

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name app.example.com;
  location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/acme; }
  location / { return 301 https://$host$request_uri; }
}

server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  server_name app.example.com;
  ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/privkey.pem;
  client_max_body_size 20m;
  gzip on; gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json;

  location /assets/ { root /srv/app; expires 7d; }
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://app;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_read_timeout 120s;
  }
}
```

Test before every reload: <code>sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx</code>

### Certificate with certbot

```
sudo apt install certbot
sudo certbot certonly --webroot -w /var/www/acme -d app.example.com
sudo certbot renew --dry-run
```

Webroot mode does not edit your nginx files.

### Rate limiting and WebSockets

```
# http {} block
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=20r/s;
map $http_upgrade $conn_upgrade { default upgrade; '' close; }

# server {} block
location /api/ { limit_req zone=api burst=40 nodelay; limit_req_status 429; proxy_pass http://app; }
location /socket.io/ {
  proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9000;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
  proxy_set_header Connection $conn_upgrade;
}
```

### Load balancing across servers

```
upstream web {
  least_conn;
  server 10.0.1.10:8000 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
  server 10.0.1.11:8000 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
  server 10.0.1.12:8000 backup;
}
```

Algorithms: round robin (default), least_conn, ip_hash (sticky).

### Troubleshoot a request end to end

```
dig +short app.example.com            # DNS
curl -sv https://app.example.com -o /dev/null   # TLS + headers
curl -w '%{time_connect} %{time_starttransfer} %{time_total}\n' -so /dev/null https://app.example.com
ss -tan state established | wc -l      # open connections
sudo tcpdump -i any port 8000 -nn -c 20
tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

### Host firewall (ufw)

```
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 80,443/tcp
sudo ufw enable && sudo ufw status verbose
```

## What we recommend

Draw the request path once for each service. When an incident happens, use it as a checklist: DNS, TLS, proxy logs, app logs, database.

